<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Ghent</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> was the peace treaty that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom, signed on 24 December 1814, in the city of Ghent, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. It restored the pre-war borders of June 1812. The five American commissioners were led by John Quincy Adams; the British at first demanded an Indigenous buffer state in the area from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, which the Americans refused to consider. News of the treaty took a month to reach the United States, and in the meantime American forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> won the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> on 8 January 1815. The treaty took effect in February 1815, when ratified copies were exchanged in Washington.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ghent.jpg" alt="Treaty of Ghent" width="330" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amédée Forestier · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Signing_of_the_Treaty_of_Ghent,_Christmas_Eve,_1814_SAAM-1922.5.2_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On Christmas Eve 1814 British and American negotiators at Ghent signed the <b>Treaty of Ghent</b>, which ended the War of 1812 and restored the pre-war borders of June 1812.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Fought after the <b>Treaty of Ghent</b> was signed but before news arrived, it made Jackson a national hero.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-8">8 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
